WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.520
Without Notch, a fly embryo builds too much nervous system and too little skin.

2
00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.400
How does one cell tell a touching neighbour, “not you”?

3
00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:14.960
Its signal never leaves the sending cell.

4
00:00:15.983 --> 00:00:19.343
Most signals you have met leave the cell that makes them.

5
00:00:20.703 --> 00:00:24.003
Notch ligands stay bolted inside the sending cell's membrane.

6
00:00:25.323 --> 00:00:29.883
The Notch receptor stays bolted inside the receiving cell right across from it.

7
00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:35.663
Because both proteins span their membranes, they usually meet only where two cell

8
00:00:35.663 --> 00:00:36.663
surfaces touch.

9
00:00:37.517 --> 00:00:42.777
Just outside the receiving membrane, Notch folds into a compact lock that hides its

10
00:00:42.777 --> 00:00:48.117
cutting spot. Now picture a loose piece of ligand floating freely in solution.

11
00:00:49.377 --> 00:00:52.097
It binds Notch with no cell holding on to pull.

12
00:00:53.377 --> 00:00:54.677
Will the receptor switch on?

13
00:01:00.583 --> 00:01:07.443
No. Binding alone leaves the lock shut, so loose ligand generally fails to switch

14
00:01:07.443 --> 00:01:13.763
Notch on. In the standard account, the sending cell draws its bound ligand inward by

15
00:01:13.763 --> 00:01:17.823
endocytosis. That tug peels the gold lock open.

16
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:23.037
Mammalian Notch gets one harmless preparation cut while it is made.

17
00:01:24.297 --> 00:01:26.117
The two halves stay clasped together.

18
00:01:27.457 --> 00:01:29.737
Now the neighbour's pull triggers the active cuts.

19
00:01:31.177 --> 00:01:36.037
First, an ADAM protease shears the newly exposed site outside the membrane.

20
00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:41.017
The outer piece departs upward with the ligand, leaving a short stub.

21
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:46.880
Next comes a cut in an unlikely place: inside the oily membrane itself.

22
00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.500
Gamma γ-Secretase accepts a receptor stub only after most of the outer part is

23
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:59.280
trimmed away. Cutting a protein needs water, but the membrane's interior is dry.

24
00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.440
So presenilin, its cutting subunit, holds its cutting site in a pocket water can

25
00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.240
reach. It snips the stub, freeing the inner tail.

26
00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:14.653
That freed piece, NICD, travels directly into the nucleus.

27
00:02:15.993 --> 00:02:21.553
In the standard model, CSL sits on target genes with co-repressors holding them off.

28
00:02:22.973 --> 00:02:27.793
NICD binds CSL, displaces co-repressors, and recruits Mastermind.

29
00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:33.133
Target genes turn on, and many make repressors that say, “not you.”

30
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:40.877
Here is the rule. A neighbour's pull lets proteases cut one Notch into one nuclear

31
00:02:40.877 --> 00:02:44.337
signal. Compare Notch with a kinase cascade.

32
00:02:45.717 --> 00:02:51.077
Watch the schematic counters: active kinases multiply the message while the receptor

33
00:02:51.077 --> 00:02:56.457
stays intact. Notch has no second messenger and no kinase relay.

34
00:02:57.837 --> 00:02:59.657
Each cut receptor is used up once.

35
00:03:00.717 --> 00:03:05.597
What the cell buys is a direct, local count of how many receptors a neighbour

36
00:03:05.597 --> 00:03:08.957
pulled. The message also stays brief.

37
00:03:10.197 --> 00:03:15.597
A nuclear kinase marks the freed tail, ubiquitin tags attach, and the proteasome

38
00:03:15.597 --> 00:03:22.237
destroys it. Once pulling stops, no fresh tails arrive, and many direct target genes

39
00:03:22.237 --> 00:03:23.077
soon go quiet.
